From e4563761299f9fcfe0d0e81ccb2dde66e70f8ab8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:39:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep your place in the grid, and let you size the thumbnails
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two fixes and one feature, across the gallery and the map panel.

Scroll position. Closing the lightbox landed you at the top of the
gallery. The cause was the body scroll lock: globals.css sets
`html, body { height: 100% }`, so `overflow: hidden` collapses the
document's scrollable area to one viewport and the browser clamps the
scroll position to zero. Lifting the overflow again doesn't bring the
number back. The lock now saves and restores it, and moves into its own
mount-only effect — folded into the keydown effect it re-ran on every
arrow press and re-read the position as zero.

The gallery then scrolls back to the photo you were last looking at,
rather than the one you opened, but only when that tile isn't already on
screen: open-then-close moves nothing.

The map panel lost its place for an unrelated reason — the lightbox
unmounts it, so the grid's scroll came back as a fresh DOM node at zero.
The position now travels in a ref owned by the page, which keeps it
across a viewer round-trip and drops it when the panel closes or another
pin is tapped.

Thumbnail size. A minus/plus control over a five-step scale, stored per
device and per grid. The size is a target tile width rather than a
column count, so one number means the same thing on a phone, on a
desktop, and in a panel the user can now drag wider; columns fall out of
`repeat(auto-fill, minmax(size, 1fr))`. The scale stops at 256px because
uploads generate a 512px thumbnail, which is exactly that on a 2x
display.

Defaults are chosen to land on the column counts the fixed classes used
to give, so nobody's grid changes shape until they ask it to.

The map panel is also resizable now: drag its left edge, arrow keys when
focused, double-click to reset.
